        "text": "We use a standard vision transformer encoder architecture (Dosovitskiy et al., 2021). We prepend the CREAM tokens with an additional learnable token zclass0 and learnable 1D positional embedding zpos is added to each token to preserve the positional information. The resultant sequence is passed as input to the encoder. The transformer consists of L encoder layers, each with MSA and MLP blocks. Each encoder layer also contains LayerNorm (LN) before every block and residual connections after every block:\nz0 = [ zclass0 ; z 1 0 ; \u00b7 \u00b7 \u00b7 ; z (p/k)2 0 ; z (p/k)2+1 0 ; z (p/k)2+2 0 ] + zpos, zpos \u2208 R((p/k) 2+3)\u00d7(pv+pd) (1)\nz\u2032\u2113 = MSA(LN (z\u2113\u22121)) + z\u2113\u22121, \u2113 = 1 . . . L (2) z\u2113 = MLP (LN (z \u2032 \u2113)) + z \u2032 \u2113, \u2113 = 1 . . . L (3)\ny = LN ( z0L ) . (4) Finally, y is linearly projected to total number of predicate relations and forms our relation classification head.\nTraining. For RGB and depth feature extraction, we follow the procedure of our baseline models (Zellers et al., 2018; Sharifzadeh et al., 2021). We train our model using the Adam optimizer (Kingma and Ba, 2015) with a batch size of 4 images and a learning rate of 1e \u2212 3. For our transformer model, we use a patch size of 2, pooled entity resolution of 8, leading to a total of 19 input tokens ( ((p/k) 2 +3) where p = 8, k = 2 ) with embedding size of 576; we employ 6 attention heads and 6 encoder layers. The model is trained for 45 epochs. We use cross-entropy as the loss function L. To compare our model against previous debiased SGG models, we use a modified cross-entropy loss that uses importance weighting for each class as proposed by Cui et al. (2019), which was also used in Zareian et al. (2020). Specifically, we use the following class balanced cross-entropy loss: LCB(p, y) = 1\u2212\u03b21\u2212\u03b2ny L(p, y), where p denotes the predicted relation probabilities, y denotes the ground-truth relation, ny the number of samples in the ground-truth relation y, and \u03b2 denotes the hyperparameter. \u03b2 = 0 would result in the standard cross-entropy loss, whereas increasing \u03b2 strenghtens the debiasing. We used the setting from Zareian et al. (2020) for our class-balanced loss."
